<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Samma dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1351 CE – 1524 CE (approx.) · capital Thatta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sindhi dynasty of jams that ruled lower Sindh from Thatta for nearly two centuries and left the vast necropolis of Makli.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Sammas, a local Sindhi Rajput-descended tribe, replaced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/soomra-dynasty">Soomra dynasty</a> in lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> around 1351. Their rulers, styled jam, acknowledged the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> at times, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq</a> campaigned against them, but they gradually established an independent state centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> delta.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-nizam-al-din-ii">Jam Nizam al-Din</a>, known as Jam Nindo, ruled for more than four decades around 1461 to 1508 and is remembered as a just and pious ruler who defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arghun">Arghuns</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thatta">Thatta</a> grew rich as a port and centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> weaving and Islamic learning. The tombs of the jams at Makli Hill, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, show a blend of local, Gujarati and Persian styles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-nizam-al-din-ii">Jam Nindo's</a> death the dynasty weakened. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-beg-arghun">Shah Beg</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/arghun-dynasty">Arghun dynasty</a>, pushed south by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and defeated the Sammas in 1520, and by 1524 Samma rule had ended.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1351 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.59%;width:3.44%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1351 CE – 1524 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thatta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jam Unar, Jam Nizam al-Din</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sindhi, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sammas, Jams of Sindh, Samma Sultanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1438&amp;focus=samma-dynasty" aria-label="Samma dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6483 1267 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Samma dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6803 1523L6832 1282"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6832 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6803 1523h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6803 1523h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Thatta. <a href="/atlas?year=1438&amp;focus=samma-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
